@*
    OdsTypeSelect — the one shared "pick a type" control behind every registry-backed
    single select (Odyssey Design System · components/TypeSelect).

    Renders the DS .odc-select trigger (a custom button, not a MudBlazor input) showing
    the chosen type's colored glyph + label, opening a MudMenu popover whose rows carry a
    colored category glyph, the label, and the selected check pinned to the far right.
    Optional Groups split the list into labelled sections with separators (e.g. Assets /
    Liabilities). Composes OdsFieldShell for the label / helper / error chrome.

    Don't use this directly in product code — reach for the domain-typed wrapper
    (OdsAccountTypeSelect, OdsContractTypeSelect, …), each of which feeds its canonical
    registry in. This is the shared engine they delegate to. Value is the enum key.

    ── The single-choice menu pattern (issue #51) ────────────────────────────────────────
    The popup is MudMenu's OWN list element and MudMenu decides its role. Up to MudBlazor
    9.9.0 that element took MudList's default role="listbox", so this was a listbox of
    role="option" rows; from 9.10.0 MudMenu passes role="menu" explicitly, so the same rows
    are now role="menuitemradio" — one radio group of choices, exactly one checked. The
    trigger's aria-haspopup moved with it, from "listbox" to "menu", because the two have to
    agree: a trigger promising a listbox that opens a menu is the mismatch this whole area
    exists to avoid.

    The rows are still rendered here rather than as MudMenuItems. The original reason held
    against 9.9.0 (a MudMenuItem carried no role and no selected state, leaving the popup a
    container with zero exposed children — WCAG 4.1.2, and 1.1.1 for the aria-hidden check
    glyph). 9.10.0 gives MudMenuItem role="menuitem", which fixes the role but still carries
    no CHECKED state, so a MudMenuItem could not say which type is chosen. Rendering here is
    what buys aria-checked, the roving-focus keyboard model below, and the DS row layout.

    Four things about that are deliberate and easy to undo by accident:

    • The container is MudMenu's own list element, so the rows are direct children of it. Do
      NOT wrap them in a role="listbox" (or any other container role): that leaves the menu
      owning no menuitems and puts a widget inside it that a menu may not own — the defect
      this fixes, one level up. The cost of using MudBlazor's element is that it takes no
      attributes of ours, so the list carries no aria-label and no id the trigger could point
      aria-controls at. Note this is a gap against the design system rather than parity with
      it: TypeSelect.jsx sets one, which it can afford to because it builds its own <ul>. It
      is not required for the pattern — the trigger's own accessible name is what names the
      control, and aria-controls is optional on a menu button.
    • A group of rows is a real role="group" named by aria-labelledby, not a bare heading.
      A presentational heading alone drops the section label out of the accessibility tree
      entirely, so a screen-reader user got one flat list where a sighted user sees Assets and
      Liabilities (WCAG 1.3.1). The heading element itself stays presentational — it is the
      group's NAME, and a stray generic child is not a valid menu descendant. role="group" is
      a valid child of both a listbox and a menu, so this survived the role change unchanged.
    • Selection is exposed with aria-checked on every row, not only on the chosen one. The
      check glyph stays aria-hidden: with aria-checked present it is decoration, and
      duplicating the state in text would make every row announce twice.
    • MudMenu contributes no navigation inside the popup: every one of its arrow, activation
      and dismissal handlers returns early while its registered-item collection is empty,
      which without MudMenuItems it always is. So the keyboard contract is implemented by
      OdsPopupMenu's single-choice mode plus the typeahead here — ↑/↓ on the trigger opens and
      focuses the selected option; ↑/↓/Home/End rove; typeahead jumps; Enter/Space select
      (natively, the options are buttons); Esc closes and restores focus to the trigger; Tab
      closes behind itself. Focus roves for real (odsFocusById) rather than via
      aria-activedescendant, matching the design system and OdsMoneyField.

    The trigger is OdsPopupMenu's own <button>, a SIBLING of the MudMenu rather than MudMenu's
    ActivatorContent (issue #255). MudMenu wraps a custom activator in a
    <div tabindex="0" role="button">, which nested this button inside a second "button", gave the
    field two tab stops, and toggled the menu a second time on every Enter/Space — the reason this
    component used to ignore keyboard-synthesised clicks. With no wrapper there is one control, it
    carries aria-expanded itself, and its native Enter/Space click is simply honoured.

    Arrow keys are not preventDefault'ed, so they still scroll the popover — Blazor evaluates
    @@onkeydown:preventDefault once at render, not per key, and a blanket preventDefault
    would trap Tab inside the list. OdsMoneyField's listbox has the same limitation.
*@

@inject IJSRuntime Js

<OdsFieldShell Label="@Label" HtmlFor="@FieldId" Required="@Required"
               Help="@Help" Error="@Error" Class="@Class">
    <div class="odc-typesel">
    @* AnchorOrigin/TransformOrigin + MudBlazor's default FlipOnOpen overflow behaviour flip the
       popover above the trigger when there isn't room below; MaxHeight caps it (DS Select/TypeSelect
       clamp) so a long list scrolls instead of running off-screen. A compact trigger (e.g. the inline
       file-kind picker) would otherwise pin the popover too narrow — it passes a PopoverClass that
       widens it to fit the option names. *@
    <OdsPopupMenu @ref="_popup"
                  TriggerId="@FieldId"
                  TriggerClass="@TriggerClass"
                  AriaLabel="@AriaLabel"
                  AriaDescribedBy="@DescribedBy"
                  AriaInvalid="@(!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Error))"
                  AnchorOrigin="Origin.BottomCenter"
                  TransformOrigin="Origin.TopCenter"
                  RelativeWidth="DropdownWidth.Relative"
                  MaxHeight="360"
                  PopoverClass="@PopoverClass"
                  Disabled="@Disabled"
                  RowId="@RowIdOf"
                  RowCount="@Ordered.Count"
                  SelectedRow="@IndexOf(Value)"
                  OpenChanged="@OnOpenChanged">
        <TriggerContent>
            <span class="odc-select-trigger-main">
                @if (Selected is not null && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(Selected.Icon))
                {
                    <span class="material-icons odc-opt-icon" style="color:@(OdsGlyphInk.Glyph(Selected.Color))" aria-hidden="true">@Selected.Icon</span>
                }
                <span class="odc-select-val">@(Selected?.Label ?? Placeholder)</span>
            </span>
            <span class="material-icons odc-select-chev" aria-hidden="true">expand_more</span>
        </TriggerContent>
        <ChildContent>
            @if (Groups is not null)
            {
                var first = true;
                var index = 0;
                foreach (var group in Groups)
                {
                    if (group.Items.Count == 0)
                    {
                        continue;
                    }
                    if (!first)
                    {
                        <div class="odc-typesel-sep" role="presentation"></div>
                    }
                    first = false;
                    var headingId = GroupId(index++);
                    <div role="group" aria-labelledby="@headingId">
                        <div class="odc-typesel-group" id="@headingId" role="presentation">@group.Label</div>
                        @foreach (var option in group.Items)
                        {
                            @OptionRow(option)
                        }
                    </div>
                }
            }
            else
            {
                foreach (var option in Types)
                {
                    @OptionRow(option)
                }
            }
        </ChildContent>
    </OdsPopupMenu>
    @if (Required)
    {
        @* A button cannot carry aria-required, and the label's `*` is aria-hidden, so the obligation
           reaches assistive technology as part of the trigger's description instead. *@
        <span id="@RequiredId" class="sr-only">Required</span>
    }
    </div>
</OdsFieldShell>

@code {
    private RenderFragment OptionRow(OdsTypeOption option) => __builder =>
    {
        var on = option.Key == Value;
        <button type="button"
                id="@OptionId(option.Key)"
                class="mud-menu-item mud-list-item-clickable odc-typesel-opt"
                role="menuitemradio"
                aria-checked="@(on ? "true" : "false")"
                tabindex="-1"
                @onclick="@(() => PickAsync(option))"
                @onkeydown="@(e => OnOptionKeyAsync(e, option))">
            <span class="mud-menu-item-text odc-typesel-item @(on ? "selected" : null)">
                <span class="material-icons odc-opt-icon" style="color:@(OdsGlyphInk.Glyph(option.Color))" aria-hidden="true">@option.Icon</span>
                <span class="odc-typesel-lab">@option.Label</span>
                @if (on)
                {
                    <span class="odc-typesel-check" aria-hidden="true"><span class="material-icons">check</span></span>
                }
            </span>
        </button>
    };
}
